script:
export const meta = {
  name: 'hail-b3a-addresses-module',
  description: 'B3 part A, the data module: website/addresses.py (streaming reader of the state address file with a field allow-list, projection, address line rules, quadtree shards, search index, words, verify, CLI), its tests, the fixture and the job step; two builders, two reviewers, skeptics, fixer, re-check',
  phases: [
    { title: 'Build', detail: 'module; tests, fixture and job step' },
    { title: 'Review', detail: 'data correctness; rules and words' },
    { title: 'Verify', detail: 'one skeptic per finding' },
    { title: 'Fix', detail: 'one fixer' },
    { title: 'Recheck', detail: 'tests, real build, measurements' },
  ],
}
const WT = '/home/user/wt/wt-b3'
const BASE = '35f1233'
const BRIEF = '/home/user/wt/briefs/hail-b3-brief.md'
const B2BRIEF = '/home/user/wt/briefs/hail-b2-brief.md'
const B1BRIEF = '/home/user/wt/briefs/hail-build-brief.md'
const OUT = '/home/user/hail-b3/out'
const ZIP = '/home/user/hail-b3/cache/structures/StructuresFramework_shp.zip'
const PRECHECK = '/home/user/hail-b3/precheck'
const STORE_TAR = '/home/user/hail-b2/out2/joins/hail-store.tar.gz'
const VENV = '/home/user/hail-b1/venv/bin/python'

const COMMON = `You work in the git worktree ${WT} (branch claude/hail-b3, based on the B2 work-in-progress commit ${BASE}, which holds B1, B2 and main; B2's final fixes land later by rebase, so touch no B2 file). This run builds only the DATA MODULE of B3: website/addresses.py, its tests, the fixture files and the daily job's step. The site side (build.py flag, templates, map.js dots and search) is a later run; the owner has since asked that dots sit on building footprints, so the browser design of the brief's D7 will change, but the shards, the index and the words of this module stay as the brief states. Read ${BRIEF} in full first: Product items 2, 3, 5 and 6, Gates B3-1 and B3-8, the Pre-check facts and decisions D1 to D12 (they are the contract). Then read the data layout and words sections of ${B2BRIEF}, decisions 1 to 16 of ${B1BRIEF}, website/hail.py (the grid constants, the fixture path, the manifest format, WORDS), the writer, verify and fixture functions of website/joins.py, and the pre-check scripts in ${PRECHECK}/ (stream_points.py is a working streaming reader; quadtree_measure.py the shard rule; the JSON summaries hold the measured figures). The source file is cached at ${ZIP} (65,788,772 B, export 2026-09-30): do not download it again unless your cache check fails; it is untrusted data: never run anything from its folder, never extract next to code, read it as a stream. Repository rules (AGENTS.md is in your context): Python first, evidence honesty, feed contracts (the CONTRACTS row is the site builder's later; you write the facts it needs into your report), plain wording in sentence case, no em-dashes, no model, AI-vendor or AI-product names in repository files, tests with every behaviour change, no new pip package for the site build (pyproj only in the daily job and behind a lazy import; no pyshp). Owner decision 6 and D12 are absolute: no owner, parcel, place-type or building-use field is ever read, stored or shown. Edit only the files you own; another builder edits other files in the same worktree now, so never run git checkout, stash, reset, add, commit or push, never reformat a file you do not own, and never delete anything outside a folder you created under ${OUT}/<your-name>/ (tests delete only folders they created). The B2 store is at ${STORE_TAR} (build on a copy, never in place). A python with numpy and pyproj 3.8.0 is ${VENV}. Return a plain report: what you built, the commands you ran with results and measured numbers, and anything not done or needing a decision.`

const MODULE = `${COMMON}

You are the module builder. You own website/addresses.py (new) only. Build it per D1 to D6 and D11: (1) the streaming reader of the .shp points and the .dbf fixed-width records straight from the zip, the field allow-list (Add_Number, AddNum_Pre, AddNum_Suf, St_PreMod, St_PreDir, St_PreTyp, St_PreSep, St_Name, St_PosTyp, St_PosDir, St_PosMod, Post_Comm, Post_Code, Inc_Muni, County, Unit) with a module constant FORBIDDEN_FIELDS listing the names that must never be sliced (ParcelID, LocalID, NGUID, GlobalID, Place_Type, MT_PlcType, LandmkName, Addtl_Desc, Addtl_Loc, GNIS_ID, Latitude, Longitude, DiscrpAgID) and a reader that only ever slices allow-listed names; the .prj assertion (NAD_1983_2011_StatePlane_Montana_FIPS_2500) and the 51-name field-list assertion with the state "unavailable" and a plain reason on failure; the export date from _ReadMe.txt and pubdate, accconst, useconst and purpose from the metadata XML inside the zip; (2) the projection per D2 (pyproj lazy import, EPSG:6514 to EPSG:4326, always_xy, 5 decimals); (3) the line, unit, city and zip rules per D3 and D4, rows without St_Name left out and exact duplicates merged, both counted; (4) the quadtree shards per D5 on hail.py's tile grid with the names and the file format of Product item 2, written under hail/a/; (5) the search index per D5 (cities.json with per-city counts and keys; letter files keyed by the first character of St_Name upper-cased, or two characters when the one-character file would exceed 2,500 rows; the slug rule) and words.json per D6; (6) the index.json "addresses" block (state, the counts and measures of D11, the source block with the file name, size, export date, pubdate and the three quotes copied from the metadata at build time) and the manifest entries in hail.py's manifest format (read and extend hail/manifest.json); (7) verify(): rows equals points minus left out minus merged; the allow-list; the three projection pairs of D2 within 2 m; manifest hashes; every shard within the limit unless at the depth cap; cities.json counts sum to the rows; 200 random rows found in their city's key file; (8) WORDS_ADDRESSES with every sentence and template of the Words section plus the unavailable sentence "Montana's address list could not be loaded in this update; that is not the same as no addresses."; describe_addresses(index) returning the About paragraphs of D8 (quotes with the file date, the allow-list sentence, the no-list-of-persons sentence, the privacy sentence, the coverage caveat: a list of addresses, not of dwellings, with the research's county ratio range 0.48 to 1.36); map_data_addresses(index) returning state, counts, the shard URL pattern, the split map (tile name to its shard names), the idx URL pattern, the words.json content, the limits (zoom 13, a tap radius of 12 px, the 20-result cap, the 2,500 limit) and every template; (9) the CLI: python -m website.addresses build|verify|fixture with --store, --cache, --zip (--zip skips the download; the download path uses the site's HTTP helper with a user agent, a ledger line and a cache keyed by file name, size and Last-Modified from a HEAD request); fixture writes a/ for the fixture tiles per D10 (real rows inside the fixture tiles' bounds plus 300 rows nearest 401 North 27th Street, Billings, whose tile joins the fixture tile list; under 50 KB in all, with cities.json, words.json and the letter files for those rows) into a given fixture folder, and the test builder wires it into the fixture. Then RUN the real build: tar -xzf ${STORE_TAR} into ${OUT}/module/store (expect hail/ with t/, j/, z/, s/, index.json, manifest.json), ${VENV} -m website.addresses build --store ${OUT}/module/store --zip ${ZIP}, then verify; measure wall time, shards, bytes of a/ and a/idx, the largest shard and letter file, the depth histogram; run the build a second time into a second store copy and diff -r the a/ trees (byte-identical expected); tar as ${OUT}/module/hail-store.tar.gz (tar czf ... -C store hail) and record its size. Report the counts against the pre-check (points 674,715; left out 4,212; merged 6,740; rows 663,763; shards 4,212; about 43.5 MB; largest about 174 KB), the three projection offsets, the field-list assertion text, and what was not done.`

const TESTS = `${COMMON}

You are the tests, fixture and job builder. You own tests/test_addresses.py (new), .github/workflows/hail-store.yml, website/data/hail-fixture/ (the a/ folder and the regenerated index.json and manifest.json; use hail.py's or joins.py's fixture path as the model and say what you ran) and, only if a one-line hook is unavoidable, website/hail.py (say exactly what changed). The module builder is writing website/addresses.py now with the public API fixed by the brief (FORBIDDEN_FIELDS, the allow-list, read_points or similar reader, build, verify, WORDS_ADDRESSES, describe_addresses, map_data_addresses, the CLI build|verify|fixture); write the tests first from the brief and D1 to D12, then poll every 2 minutes (up to 45 minutes) for the module and adapt the names to what it exports, keeping the brief's semantics. tests/test_addresses.py: a synthetic zip the test writes itself in a folder it creates (a tiny .shp with point records, .shx, a .dbf with the 51 field names of the pre-check in order and the right lengths, the .prj text, _ReadMe.txt with an export date line, a metadata XML with the three quoted sentences) holding rows that exercise: a blank street, an exact duplicate, a unit "UNIT A" and a bare "A", a blank Post_Comm with Inc_Muni "Chinook" and one with "Unincorporated" and County "Glacier County", a 6-character zip, an AddNum_Suf "1/2", a St_PreTyp "United States Highway" with a numeric St_Name "212", a St_PreDir "North" with St_Name "27th" and St_PosTyp "Street"; then assert: the allow-list excludes every FORBIDDEN_FIELDS name and the reader slices nothing else (inspect the slice table), the line rules of D3, the city and zip rules of D4, the left-out and merged counts, the quadtree split and names on synthetic dense points (more than 2,500 rows in one tile split into quarters with the right digits and bounds), the index keys and the two-character split, the slug, the unavailable state on a changed field list and on a changed .prj, the forbidden-term check over WORDS_ADDRESSES and describe_addresses (owner, resident, lives, tenant, occupant, parcel, value, sale, mortgage, damage, claim, insurance, eligible, confirmed, all clear, no hail; the quoted metadata sentences and the About's own exclusion sentence excepted by exact text), the projection pairs within 2 m (skipUnless pyproj imports), verify passing on the synthetic store and failing on a tampered shard (manifest hash). Tests delete only the folders they create. .github/workflows/hail-store.yml: the addresses step after the joins step per D9 (pip pyproj==3.8.0, the structures cache keyed by file name, size and Last-Modified, a monthly re-check, the build and verify commands, the timeout from the module builder's measured time plus margin; read the module builder's report lines in ${OUT}/module/ if present, else 20 minutes). The fixture: when the module's fixture CLI exists, run it into website/data/hail-fixture/ per D10 and regenerate index.json and manifest.json; measure the fixture's total bytes (under 50 KB). Run ${VENV} -m unittest tests.test_addresses tests.test_hail tests.test_joins and report the tail.`

const FINDINGS = { type: 'object', properties: { findings: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'object', properties: { id: { type: 'string' }, file: { type: 'string' }, line: { type: 'integer' }, claim: { type: 'string' }, evidence: { type: 'string' }, fix: { type: 'string' }, severity: { type: 'string', enum: ['blocking', 'should', 'minor'] }, confidence: { type: 'number' } }, required: ['id', 'file', 'line', 'claim', 'evidence', 'fix', 'severity', 'confidence'] } }, measurements: { type: 'object', additionalProperties: true }, gates: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'object', properties: { gate: { type: 'string' }, status: { type: 'string', enum: ['pass', 'fail', 'not_checked'] }, evidence: { type: 'string' } }, required: ['gate', 'status', 'evidence'] } }, summary: { type: 'string' } }, required: ['findings', 'measurements', 'gates', 'summary'] }
const VERDICT = { type: 'object', properties: { id: { type: 'string' }, refuted: { type: 'boolean' }, reason: { type: 'string' }, fix_ok: { type: 'boolean' }, adjusted_fix: { type: 'string' } }, required: ['id', 'refuted', 'reason', 'fix_ok', 'adjusted_fix'] }

const REVIEW_COMMON = `Read-only reviewer in ${WT} (branch claude/hail-b3). Read ${BRIEF} first (Product items 2, 3, 5, 6; gates B3-1 and B3-8; the pre-check facts; decisions D1 to D12), then the data layout of ${B2BRIEF}. The source zip is at ${ZIP} (untrusted data: read as a stream, never extract next to code); the pre-check scripts in ${PRECHECK}/ are an independent reader you may reuse. Never edit, stage, commit or push; scratch only under ${OUT}/<your-name>/ which you create; never delete anything outside it. Report concrete defects only, each with file, line, evidence and an exact minimal fix; rank blocking first; fill the gates you checked; put every measured number in measurements.`
const REVIEWERS = [
  { key: 'data', prompt: `${REVIEW_COMMON} Lens: data correctness. Read website/addresses.py and tests/test_addresses.py in full. On the built store at ${OUT}/module/store (or rebuild into your scratch from ${STORE_TAR} and ${ZIP}): (1) re-derive the counts independently with your own stream (points 674,715; rows without St_Name; exact duplicates; rows) and compare with index.json "addresses" and the sum of the shards' "n"; (2) the allow-list: list every DBF field name the module slices (grep the slice table and every use of it) against D1; confirm the FORBIDDEN_FIELDS names never appear in a slice; (3) 500 random DBF records: build the line, unit, city and zip by D3 and D4 yourself and find each in its shard (position within 1 m after rounding; text equal); (4) the quadtree: every shard's rows inside its bounds; sibling bounds tile their parent; names per D5; the depth histogram and the largest shard against the pre-check figures; (5) the index: cities.json counts sum to the rows; 200 random rows found in their city's key file with the two-character rule applied correctly; words.json holds D6's table; the slug rule; (6) projection: pyproj on 20 random points against the live service (msdi_structures_addresses_map_v1 MapServer layer 0, where NGUID equals the record's, outSR=4326; the NGUID is read for this check only in your scratch) within 2 m; (7) determinism: two builds byte-identical in a/; (8) verify runs and passes; manifest hashes match; (9) the unavailable path: a zip with one field renamed gives state unavailable with a reason and no a/ files; (10) the quotes in index.json's source block equal the metadata XML's text.` },
  { key: 'rules', prompt: `${REVIEW_COMMON} Lens: repository rules and words over the whole diff (git -C ${WT} diff ${BASE} --stat, the full diff, untracked files). Check: decision 6 and D12 (no owner, parcel, place-type or building-use field read, stored or shown; grep the module and the built a/ files for owner, resident, tenant, occupant, parcel outside FORBIDDEN_FIELDS and the About exclusion sentence); evidence honesty in WORDS_ADDRESSES and describe_addresses (no damage, all-clear, insurance, eligibility; the unavailable sentence present; the coverage caveat present); the terms quotes exact against the metadata XML inside ${ZIP}; tests exist for every rule of D1 to D6 and ${VENV} -m unittest discover -s tests passes (quote the tail) and python desktop/launcher.py --selftest payload passes; the job step is sound (order after joins, pyproj pinned, the cache key, the monthly re-check, the timeout against the measured time) and the YAML parses; em-dashes in added lines 0; the words Claude, Anthropic, Codex, ChatGPT, OpenAI, Copilot, Gemini, Haiku, Opus, Sonnet in added lines only inside branch names; plain wording and sentence case; no new pip package for the site build (pyproj only behind a lazy import; no pyshp); the fixture under 50 KB and its index.json and manifest.json consistent (hashes); decision 7 audit: every rmtree, unlink or os.remove under tests/ acts on a folder the test created; no file from the zip written next to code; no B2 file changed except the fixture's index.json and manifest.json.` },
]
const OWN = ['website/addresses.py', 'tests/test_addresses.py', '.github/workflows/hail-store.yml', 'website/data/hail-fixture/', 'website/hail.py']

phase('Build')
const [moduleR, testsR] = await parallel([
  () => agent(MODULE, { label: 'build:module', phase: 'Build', model: 'haiku' }),
  () => agent(TESTS, { label: 'build:tests-fixture-job', phase: 'Build', model: 'haiku' }),
])
log(`builders done: module ${moduleR ? 'ok' : 'null'}, tests ${testsR ? 'ok' : 'null'}`)

phase('Review')
const reviews = await pipeline(
  REVIEWERS,
  (r) => agent(`${r.prompt}\n\nBuilder reports for context (claims to check, not facts):\nMODULE: ${(moduleR || 'none').slice(0, 7000)}\nTESTS: ${(testsR || 'none').slice(0, 5000)}`, { label: `review:${r.key}`, phase: 'Review', schema: FINDINGS, model: 'haiku' }),
  async (result, r) => {
    if (!result) return { key: r.key, findings: [], measurements: {}, gates: [], summary: 'reviewer returned nothing' }
    const fs = result.findings.map((f, i) => ({ ...f, id: `${r.key}-${i + 1}` }))
    log(`${r.key}: ${fs.length} findings`)
    const verdicts = await parallel(fs.map((f) => () =>
      agent(`You are a skeptic checking one review finding in ${WT} (read-only; never edit, stage, commit or push; scratch only under ${OUT}/skeptic-${f.id}/ which you create). The finding:\n${JSON.stringify(f, null, 1)}\n\nOpen the file at the line, reproduce the observation yourself, and read ${BRIEF} (decisions D1 to D12). The finding stands only if the defect is real, matters to gates B3-1 or B3-8 or the repository rules (AGENTS.md), and the proposed fix is correct and minimal and introduces nothing unsupported (no new number, no em-dash, no model or AI-product name, no new pip package for the site build, no owner or parcel field, no contradiction of the brief's decisions). If the defect is real but the fix is wrong or too broad, set fix_ok false and give an adjusted_fix. Default to refuted=true when uncertain.`,
        { label: `verify:${f.id}`, phase: 'Verify', schema: VERDICT, model: 'haiku' }).then((v) => (v ? { ...f, verdict: v } : null))))
    return { key: r.key, findings: verdicts.filter(Boolean), measurements: result.measurements, gates: result.gates, summary: result.summary }
  },
)
const all = reviews.flatMap((r) => r.findings)
const confirmed = all.filter((f) => !f.verdict.refuted).map((f) => ({ id: f.id, file: f.file, line: f.line, claim: f.claim, evidence: f.evidence, severity: f.severity, fix: f.verdict.fix_ok ? f.fix : f.verdict.adjusted_fix })).filter((f) => f.fix && f.fix.length > 0)
const refuted = all.filter((f) => f.verdict.refuted).map((f) => ({ id: f.id, file: f.file, claim: f.claim, reason: f.verdict.reason }))
log(`confirmed ${confirmed.length} (blocking ${confirmed.filter((f) => f.severity === 'blocking').length}), refuted ${refuted.length}`)

phase('Fix')
const fixR = confirmed.length ? await agent(`${COMMON}\n\nYou are the fixer; you own exactly: ${OWN.join(', ')}. Apply these confirmed findings with exact, minimal edits that keep every public name and the brief's contract (decisions D1 to D12 decide any choice; a finding that contradicts one is skipped with the decision cited). Skip and report any finding naming another file. After editing run ${VENV} -m unittest tests.test_addresses tests.test_hail tests.test_joins; if a fix changes the a/ formats, the index or the rows, rebuild ${OUT}/module/store from ${STORE_TAR} and ${ZIP} and re-tar ${OUT}/module/hail-store.tar.gz, and say so. Report applied ids, skipped ids with reasons, and the test tail.\n\nFINDINGS:\n${JSON.stringify(confirmed, null, 1)}`, { label: 'fix', phase: 'Fix', model: 'haiku' }) : 'no confirmed findings'

phase('Recheck')
const recheck = await agent(`${REVIEW_COMMON} You are the final checker of this data-module run. Run and quote: ${VENV} -m unittest discover -s tests (tail); python desktop/launcher.py --selftest payload; ${VENV} -m website.addresses verify on ${OUT}/module/store (rebuild it first from ${STORE_TAR} and ${ZIP} into ${OUT}/recheck/store if the fixer said formats changed, and compare); index.json "addresses" counts against the pre-check (points 674,715; left out 4,212; merged 6,740; rows 663,763; shards 4,212; about 43.5 MB; largest about 174 KB) with differences explained; du -sb of hail/a and hail/a/idx; the largest shard and letter file; the tar size; the fixture bytes; git -C ${WT} status --short and diff --stat ${BASE}; em-dash and banned-name counts over added lines; the decision 7 audit; the allow-list grep. Write ${OUT}/b3a-recheck.json with gates B3-1 and B3-8 (status, evidence), the measurements, and the applied and refuted findings (ids, one line each). Return that JSON as text.`, { label: 'recheck', phase: 'Recheck', model: 'haiku' })

return { moduleR: (moduleR || '').slice(0, 8000), testsR: (testsR || '').slice(0, 5000), reviews: reviews.map((r) => ({ key: r.key, summary: r.summary, gates: r.gates, measurements: r.measurements })), confirmed, refuted, fixR, recheck }
